    "title": "How Does Wright State University Rank Among America's Best Colleges?",
    "text": "Wright State University is a public institution that was founded in 1967. In the 2026 edition of Best Colleges, Wright State University is ranked No. #395-434 in National Universities. It's also ranked No. #426 in Top Performers on Social Mobility. It has a total undergraduate enrollment of 7,505 (fall 2024), and the campus size is 651 acres. The student-faculty ratio at Wright State University is 16:1, and it utilizes a semester-based academic calendar. Wright State University has a test-optional admissions policy. The school's in-state tuition and fees are $10,991; out-of-state tuition and fees are $20,691. Fifty-nine percent of first-year students receive need-based financial aid, and the average net price for federal loan recipients is $15,933. The four-year graduation rate is 28%. Six years after graduation, the median salary for graduates is $42,229.Wright State University is a public institution that was founded in 1967. In the 2026 edition of Best Colleges, Wright State University is ranked No. #395-434 in National Universities. It's also ranked No. #426 in Top Performers on Social Mobility. It has a total undergraduate enrollment of 7,505 (fall 2024), and the campus size is 651 acres. The student-faculty ratio at Wright State University is 16:1, and it utilizes a semester-based academic calendar. Wright State University has a test-optional admissions policy. The school's in-state tuition and fees are $10,991; out-of-state tuition and fees are $20,691. Fifty-nine percent of first-year students receive need-based financial aid, and the average net price for federal loan recipients is $15,933. The four-year graduation rate is 28%. Six years after graduation, the median salary for graduates is $42,229.",
